own that the expected value and variance of $X$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{a+b}{2} \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =\\frac{(b-a)^{2}}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result for $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is intuitive as it's simply the midpoint of the interval $[a,b].$ \n\nWe will derive both of these results at the end of the lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168086 #edb/ref 1065 5171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168086 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b5aec157-2481-5e36-90cf-d794324c0d63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168086 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168111 #edb/ref 1064 "If $X\\sim U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n![](images/be/be9457e598579e35be37d8fcc86663f4229e6193f02b11c49242d63858406a3b.svg)\n\nWe can compute the formula for $\\textrm E[X]$ using the usual definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}xf(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{x}{b-a}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{b-a}\\cdot {\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}|}_{a}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(b-a)}\\cdot ({b}^{2}-{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(b-a)}\\cdot (b-a)(b+a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cancel{(b-a)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(b-a)}(b+a) \\\\  & =\\frac{a+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168111 #edb/ref 1065 5374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168111 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1bdf6fbe-b1ad-56fa-a9f6-e9f99b79f3d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168111 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Formula for the Mean" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168112 #edb/ref 1064 "If $X\\sim U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n![](images/57/579372cf7b7880117ff6e7b4e1f063b29e42f886b06e2277e2dcb1f1240ff11d.svg)\n\nTo compute $\\textrm{Var}[X],$ we use the relation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - (\\textrm{E}[X])^2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\textrm E[X^2]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}{x}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{b-a}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{b-a}\\cdot {\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}|}_{a}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(b-a)}\\cdot ({b}^{3}-{a}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(b-a)}\\cdot (b-a)({b}^{2}+ab+{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cancel{(b-a)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(b-a)}({b}^{2}+ab+{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({b}^{2}+ab+{a}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\textrm E[X] = \\dfrac12(b+a),$ we have\n\n$$\n(\\textrm E[X])^2 = \\dfrac14(b^2+2ab+a^2).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-(\\text{E}[X])^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({b}^{2}+ab+{a}^{2})-\\frac{1}{4}({b}^{2}+2ab+{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{12}({b}^{2}+ab+{a}^{2})-\\frac{3}{12}({b}^{2}+2ab+{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(4{b}^{2}+4ab+4{a}^{2})-\\frac{1}{12}(3{b}^{2}+6ab+3{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}({b}^{2}-2ab+{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(b-a)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168112 #edb/ref 1065 5375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168112 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "16d85ab9-dd16-51a2-97f0-2ca5ef2e3d08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168112 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Formula for the Variance" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542618 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186543598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542618 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186543636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542618 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186543642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542618 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186543643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542618 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186543652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542618 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542618 #edb/ref 1045 "Mean and Variance of the Continuous Uniform Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542618 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186543642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542618 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref